//Iterative Approach

#BRUTEFORCE APPROACH (Data Replacement)

Key- Idea :
- Traverse the linked list and push each node's value onto a stack.
- Reset temp = head to start from the first node again.
- Traverse the list again and pop values from the stack to overwrite each node's value in reverse order.
- Return head.
Time Complexity: \(O(n)\) — Two traversals of the linked list, each taking \(O(n)\).

Space Complexity: \(O(n)\) — The stack stores all \(n\) node values.

CODE:=>

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode next;
 *     ListNode() {}
 *     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
        ListNode temp = head;
        Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

        //store data
        while(temp != null) {
            st.push(temp.val);
            temp = temp.next;
        }
        //move temp to start head node
        temp = head;

        //rewrite data
        while(temp != null) {
            temp.val = st.pop();
            temp = temp.next;
        }
        return head;
    }
}



#OPTIMIZED APPROACH  (Links Arrangement)

Key Idea (Iterative Reversal – Three Pointers)
- Initialize prev = null, temp = head, and use next to save the next node.
- Store temp.next in next so the remaining list is not lost.
- Reverse the current link using temp.next = prev.
- Move prev to temp and temp to next.
- Repeat until temp == null. At this point, prev is the new head of the reversed list.
Time Complexity: \(O(n)\) — Each node is visited once.

Space Complexity: \(O(1)\) — Only three pointer variables are used.


CODE:=>
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
        ListNode temp = head;
        ListNode prev = null;
        ListNode next ;

        //arranging links
        while(temp != null) {
            next = temp.next;
            temp.next = prev;
            prev = temp;
            temp = next;
        }

        return prev;
    }
}




//REcursive Approach

#Optimized solution!

Key Idea (Recursive Reversal)
- Base case: If head == null or head.next == null, return head because an empty or single-node list is already reversed.
- Recursive call: reverseList(head.next) reverses the remaining linked list and returns its new head.
- Reverse the link: Set front = head.next, then front.next = head to point the next node back to the current node.
- Break the old link: Set head.next = null to avoid creating a cycle.
- Return: newHead, which points to the first node of the reversed list.
Time Complexity: \(O(n)\) — Each node is processed once.

Space Complexity: \(O(n)\) — The recursion call stack can grow up to \(n\) calls.


CODE: => 

class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
        //base case
        if(head == null || head.next == null) {
            return head;
        }

        //actual recursive flow
        ListNode front;

        ListNode newHead = reverseList(head.next);
        front = head.next;
        front.next = head;
        head.next = null;

        return newHead;
    }
